<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Salm</h1><p class="st-meta">1623 CE – 1793 CE (approx.) · capital Senones</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Vosges principality of the Salm family around the abbey town of Senones, which voted to join France in 1793.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Salm, a branch of the Wild- and Rhinegraves, held lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senones">Senones</a> and Badonviller. In 1623 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-otto-of-salm">Philip Otto of Salm</a> was made a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and the territory became a principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a small mountain land of forests, ironworks and later textile workshops. Its life centred on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/senones">Senones</a> and its Benedictine abbey, whose abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustin-calmet">Dom Augustin Calmet</a> was a celebrated scholar. In 1751 the territory was reorganised and Senones became the princes' residence, with a modest court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">Revolutionary France</a> and cut off by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, the inhabitants voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> annexed the principality in 1793. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salm-1802">Salm-Salm</a> princes received compensation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-salm.jpg" alt="Principality of Salm" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salm-Salm.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1623 CE – 1793 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Senones</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip Otto of Salm, Nicholas Leopold of Salm-Salm</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Salm-Salm, Principality of Salm-Salm</dd></dl></aside></div>
